ore on  battery in just a bit the quad speakers  are user-facing with Dolby tuning and  sound above average for an ultra-light  Windows laptop surprising 2.6 pounds in  this case but it can't keep up with  Apple's $1100 MacBook Air  or similarly priced 13-inch MacBook Pros  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  at the center is probably the best  feature of the laptop and that is the  backlit keyboard the key travel is just  long enough to feel great but still stay  thin and with enough pressure required  to eliminate mist types without feeling  too clicky and with dampening without  being too mushy if you type a lot this  is one of the best keyboards and any  laptop that is this thin in light on the  sides we have a headphone mic combo jack  along with three ports to being full  speed Thunder both three and the other  USB type-c but for some reason it's only  USB see gen1 meaning that the speed is  only half of what it could be now being  macbook fans we're used to only having  USB type-c ports but it still is a bit  disappointing to see a $2000 laptop with  less ports than apple's equivalent that  gets so much hate for being a dongle  book our s 940 has 512 gigabytes of nvme  SSD running at a super-fast 3341  megabytes per second read and 1939  megabytes per second write which is  refreshing compared to other ultra books  that skimp on SSDs so far this lenovo is  sounding very good overall but this is  where I have to get to the few weak  spots of this beautiful ultra book and  even though there's not that many of  them to clean about these can really  make or break your decision first off  the webcam both looks poor and sounds  really muffled this is an audio and  video quality test on the lenovo ideapad  s 940 second is the battery life it  makes sense that lenovo could only fit a  52 watt hour battery in this ultra-thin  machine but pairing it to a 500 nits 4k  display results in really poor battery  life lenovo rates it at seven-and-a-half  hours of battery life which in the real  world is closer to 5 hours with usable  display brightness levels if you want to  go with a very color accurate gorgeous  HDR displays this places it at the  bottom of the market for battery life  now opting for the 1080p display will  about double battery life  that's a big trade-off since it's not as  bright and it's much lower quality  overall personally we wish they would  have split it right in the middle like  Huawei or Apple with the resolution kind  of between 1080p and a 4k that will  still give you a detailed image and ten  hours of battery life while maintaining  500 nits of brightness but instead you  have to choose from a top-notch bright  4k display with poor battery life or an  average 1080p display with good battery  life and that choice is hard to make the  last downside is the performance the  spec sheet sounds very promising with an  HM quad-core 1.8 gigahertz i7 processor  that can boost all the way up to 4.6  gigahertz but running a few tests gave  us disappointing results the similarly  priced 13-inch MacBook Pro scored about  40 percent higher in Geekbench 4 and  almost 60 percent higher in Cinebench  r20 where all of the cores are maxed out  even though the MacBook is an i5 CPU not  an i7 even comparing it to a $1,300 base  1.4 gigahertz I 5 macbook pro the lenovo  s 940 falls really far behind how is  that possible you may ask well the CPUs  and both these machines are rated at 15  watts but the lenovo is not letting the  i7 run higher than that where the  MacBook can continuously run at about 30  watts and yes that's what all of the  power saving features turned off and  with the s 940 plugged in while the  MacBook Pros are on battery power  without having to find and change any  power options other Windows laptops  don't limit the CPU as much but I could  see why lenovo is doing this given the  power draw of the brag 4k display and  because of the smaller battery capacity  processor performance isn't the only  downside but the Intel UHD graphic 620  did struggle a bit with the 4k display  causing occasional UI stutters and also  sometimes when playing back 4k YouTube  videos of course the same GPU is used in  most Windows equivalents but most of  those have lower resolutions and the  1080p version of the s 940 shouldn't  have these issues now on the Mac side  Apple pays more for Intel chips with  iris graphics which provide more than  double the performance as you can see  here in our Geekbench OpenCL test these  downsides paired with  $2,000 MSRP make the s 940 a laptop  that's hard to recommend even though we  really like a lot about it this is a  really nice aluminum ultra-thin and  light machine with only USB type-c ports  and a gorgeous display at a premium  price almost like you're buying a  Windows version of a macbook we did  decide by side comparison that you can  check out by using a card at the end of  this video and the MacBook Pro has much  better performance has four Thunderbolt  3 ports much better speakers and  trackpad a better webcam it has touch ID  instead of Windows hello and about  double the battery life while having an  almost a sharp display with better color  accuracy and similar brightness you  don't have HDR for videos and the  keyboard is a ways behind but overall it  provides a much better value for the  price now the main reason why we're  comparing it to a macro pro is because  lenovo prioritized many things that  Apple does and at a similar price point  but of course not everybody wants a Mac  and that's where the new XPS 95 80 or  razor blade stealth come in both having  better port options improved performance  and a lower regular price which make the  s 940 a hard sell but there is one  redeeming factor and that's the good  discounts that Lenovo offers on their  own website which we will link down in  the description  prices do change me right now the $1,800  model is 360 dollars off which is still  only $60 cheaper than a similarly  equipped MacBook Pro but at least at  that price I can actually recommend it  to one group of people those who do a  lot of typing for most people even at  that price a MacBook or XPS is probably  a better choice but if you prioritize  design and want a thin and light Windows  laptop that has a gorgeous screen to  look out while you type away at the top  notch keyboard for up to five hours at a  time the lenovo s 940 will be a  fantastic laptop for your needs but if  you care about performance battery life  ports and value more than you do design  then you should look elsewhere thank you  guys for watching this has been max max  tech go ahead and click that little  circle above if you guys want to  subscribe and enable those notifications  below and if you want to see our full  comparison of the lenovo s 940 against  the similar price macbook pro you guys  can click right  for their thanks for watching and I will  see you guys in the next video  [Music]
 

